2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Additional funding provided by

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
The National Center for the Arts and Sciences

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,000
and the National Center for the Arts and Sciences.

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:21,000
The National Center for the Arts and Sciences

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
is a non-profit, non-profit organization

7
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,000
based in the United States.

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:33,000
When we finished last time,

9
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
we were looking at Jon Stewart Mills' attempt

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
to create a program

11
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,000
to reply to the critics

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
of Bentham's utilitarianism.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:50,000
In his book Utilitarianism,

14
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Mills tries to show that critics to the contrary,

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
it is possible within the utilitarian framework

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
to distinguish between higher and lower pleasures.

17
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,000
It is possible to make qualitative distinctions of worth,

18
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:09,000
and we tested that idea.

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
With the Simpsons and the Shakespeare excerpts.

20
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
And the results of our experiment

21
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
seem to call into question

22
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Mills' distinction.

23
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Because a great many of you

24
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
reported that you prefer the Simpsons,

25
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
but that you still consider Shakespeare

26
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,000
to be the higher or the worthier.

27
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,000
Pleasure.

28
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,000
That's the dilemma

29
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
with which our experiment confronts Mill.

30
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
What about Mills' attempt to account

31
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:54,000
for the specially weighty character

32
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
of individual rights and justice

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:59,000
in chapter five of Utilitarianism?

34
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
He wants to say that individual rights

35
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
are worthy of special respect.

36
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
In fact, he goes so far as to say

37
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
that justice is the most sacred part

38
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:16,000
and the most incomparably binding part of morality.

39
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
But the same challenge

40
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
could be put to this part of Mills' defense.

41
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Why is justice the chief part

42
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:32,000
and the most binding part of all morality?

43
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Well, he says because in the long run,

44
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:38,000
if we do justice and if we respect rights,

45
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,500
society as a whole will be

46
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:43,000
better off in the long run.

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
Well, what about that?

48
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,000
What if we have a case where making an exception

49
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,000
and violating individual rights

50
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
actually will make people better off in the long run?

51
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Is it all right then to use people?

52
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,000
And there's a further objection

53
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:04,000
that could be raised against

54
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Mills' case for justice and rights.

55
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Suppose the Utilitarianism

56
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,000
and the Utilitarianism of the United States

57
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,000
is to be respected.

58
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Suppose the Utilitarian Calculus in the long run

59
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:15,000
works out as he says it will,

60
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:18,000
such that respecting people's rights

61
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
is a way of making everybody better off

62
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:23,000
in the long run.

63
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Is that the right reason?

64
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Is that the only reason to respect people?

65
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
If the doctor goes in and yanks the organs

66
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
from the healthy patient who came in for a checkup

67
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and then he says,

68
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
well, I'm going to use him in this way,

69
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:40,000
in the long run,

70
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
more lives will be lost.

71
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
There would be adverse effects in the long run.

72
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,000
Eventually, people would learn about this

73
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and would stop going in for checkups.

74
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
Is it the right reason?

75
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
Is the only reason that you as the doctor

76
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
won't yank the organs out of the healthy patient

77
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,000
that you think,

78
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
well, if I use him in this way,

79
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:07,000
he'll be out?

80
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Or is there another reason,

81
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
having to do with intrinsic respect

82
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:13,000
for the person as an individual?

83
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And if that reason matters,

84
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
then it's not so clear

85
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,000
that even Mill's utilitarianism

86
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
can take account of it.

87
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Fully to examine these two worries or objections

88
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
to Mill's defense,

89
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
we need to do,

90
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:36,000
and push further,

91
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:38,000
and we need to ask,

92
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
in the case of higher or worthier pleasures,

93
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,000
are there theories of the good life

94
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
that can provide independent moral standards

95
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:54,000
for the worth of pleasures?

96
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:56,000
If so, what do they look like?

97
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,000
That's one question.

98
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
In the case of justice and rights,

99
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
if we suspect that Mill

100
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
is implicitly leaning on notions of human dignity

101
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:08,000
or respect for a person

102
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
that are not, strictly speaking, utilitarian,

103
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,000
we need to look to see

104
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
whether there are some stronger theories of rights

105
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:21,000
that can explain the intuition,

106
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:23,000
which even Mill shares,

107
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
the intuition that the reason

108
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
for respecting individuals and not using them

109
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
goes beyond even utility

110
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,000
in the long run.

111
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:39,500
Today we turn to one

112
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:43,000
of those strong theories of rights.

113
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Strong theories of rights say

114
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
individuals matter

115
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:51,000
not just as instruments to be used

116
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
for a larger social purpose

117
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
or for the sake of maximizing utility.

118
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:01,000
Individuals are separate beings

119
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:03,000
with separate lives,

120
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:06,000
worthy of respect.

121
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:08,000
And so it's a mistake,

122
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:10,000
according to strong theories of rights,

123
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:12,000
it's a mistake

124
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:17,000
to think about justice or law

125
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
by just adding up preferences and values.

126
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,000
The strong rights theory we turn to today

127
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:27,000
is libertarianism.

128
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Libertarianism takes individual rights seriously.

129
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,000
It's called libertarianism because it says

130
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:38,000
the fundamental individual right

131
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:41,000
is the right to liberty.

132
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:47,000
Precisely because we are separate individual beings,

133
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:50,000
we're not available

134
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:52,000
to any use

135
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:55,000
that the society might desire or devise.

136
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Precisely because we are individual,

137
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:00,000
separate human beings,

138
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
we have a fundamental right to liberty.

139
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
And that means

140
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,000
a right to choose freely,

141
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:12,000
to live our lives as we please,

142
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:15,000
provided we respect other people's rights

143
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:17,000
to do the same.

144
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:19,000
That's the fundamental idea.

145
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,000
Robert Nozick,

146
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:25,000
one of the libertarian philosophers we read

147
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:28,000
for this course, puts it this way.

148
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Individuals have rights

149
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,000
so strong and far-reaching

150
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,000
are these rights

151
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
that they raise the question of what,

152
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:37,000
if anything,

153
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:43,000
the state may do.

154
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:46,000
So what does libertarianism say

155
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:49,000
about the role of government

156
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,000
or of the state?

157
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:53,000
Well, there are three things

158
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:57,000
that most modern states do

159
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:01,000
that, on the libertarian theory of rights,

160
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:03,000
are illegitimate,

161
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:05,000
are unjust.

162
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:09,000
One of them is paternalist legislation.

163
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:10,000
That's passing laws

164
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
that protect people from themselves.

165
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
Seatbelt laws, for example,

166
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:18,000
or motorcycle helmet laws.

167
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,000
The libertarian says,

168
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:21,000
it may be a good thing

169
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:23,000
if people wear seatbelts,

170
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,000
but that should be up to them.

171
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:29,000
And the state, the government,

172
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:32,000
has no business coercing them,

173
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:38,000
us, to wear seatbelts by law.

174
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
It's coercion.

175
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:43,000
So no paternalist legislation,

176
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:44,000
number one.

177
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:48,000
Number two, no morals legislation.

178
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,000
Many laws try to promote

179
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,000
the virtue of citizens

180
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:56,000
or try to give expression

181
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,000
to the moral values

182
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
of the society as a whole.

183
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:04,000
Libertarians say,

184
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:06,000
that's also a violation

185
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:09,000
of the right to liberty.

186
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Take the example of,

187
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:13,000
well, a classic example

188
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:14,000
of legislation offered

189
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,000
in the name of promoting morality.

190
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Traditionally,

191
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:20,000
have been laws that prevent

192
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:22,000
sexual intimacy

193
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
between gays and lesbians.

194
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
The libertarian says,

195
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,000
nobody else is harmed,

196
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:32,000
nobody else's rights are violated,

197
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:35,000
so the state should get out

198
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:37,000
of the business entirely

199
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
of trying to promote virtue

200
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
or to enact morals legislation.

201
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
And the third kind of law

202
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
or policy that is ruled out

203
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
on the libertarian philosophy

204
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
is any taxation

205
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
or other policy

206
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:00,000
that serves the purpose

207
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:04,000
of redistributing income or wealth

208
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:07,000
from the rich to the poor.

209
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
Redistribution is a kind,

210
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
if you think about it,

211
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:11,000
says the libertarian,

212
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:15,000
is a kind of coercion.

213
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
What it amounts to

214
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:19,000
is theft

215
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,000
by the state

216
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:22,000
or by the majority,

217
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:25,000
if we're talking about a democracy,

218
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,000
from people who happen to do

219
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
very well and earn a lot of money.

220
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,000
Now,

221
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Nozick and other libertarians

222
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:38,000
allow that there can be a minimal state

223
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:41,000
that taxes people for the sake of

224
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
what everybody needs,

225
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:45,000
the national defense,

226
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:46,000
police force,

227
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:47,000
judicial system

228
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,000
to enforce contracts

229
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
and property rights,

230
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
but that's it.

231
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
Now,

232
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
I want to get your reactions

233
00:10:56,000 --> 00:11:00,000
to this third feature

234
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
of the libertarian view.

235
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
I want to see

236
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
who among you

237
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:07,000
agree with that idea

238
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,000
and who disagree and why.

239
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
But just to make it concrete

240
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
and to see what's at stake,

241
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:17,000
consider the distribution of wealth

242
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
in the United States.

243
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
The United States is among the most

244
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:23,000
inegalitarian societies

245
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:26,000
as far as the distribution of wealth,

246
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:29,000
of all the advanced democracies.

247
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:30,000
Now,

248
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:31,000
is this just

249
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:33,000
or unjust?

250
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Well, what does the libertarian say?

251
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:39,000
The libertarian says,

252
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
you can't know just from the facts

253
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
I've just given you,

254
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
you can't know whether that distribution

255
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:48,000
is just or unjust.

256
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
You can't know just by looking at a pattern

257
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:53,000
or a distribution or a result

258
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,000
whether it's just or unjust.

259
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
You have to know how it came to be.

260
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
You can't just look at the end state

261
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
or the result.

262
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:08,000
You have to look at two principles.

263
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,000
The first he calls justice in acquisition

264
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
or in initial holdings.

265
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
And what that means simply is,

266
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:19,000
did people get the things they used

267
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:22,000
to make their money

268
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
fairly?

269
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
So we need to know

270
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
was there justice in the initial holdings?

271
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
Did they steal the land or the factory

272
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
or the goods that enabled them to make

273
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:34,000
all that money?

274
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:35,000
If not,

275
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
if they were entitled to whatever it was

276
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
that enabled them to

277
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
gather the wealth,

278
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
the first principle is met.

279
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
The second principle is,

280
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
did the distribution arise

281
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:51,000
from the operation of free consent?

282
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:54,000
People buying and trading on the market.

283
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:55,000
As you can see,

284
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
the libertarian idea of justice

285
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:00,000
corresponds to a free market

286
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
conception of justice.

287
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:06,000
Provided people got what they used

288
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,000
fairly, didn't steal it,

289
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:12,000
and provided the distribution results

290
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
from the free choice of individuals

291
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:17,000
buying and selling things,

292
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
the distribution is just.

293
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:22,000
And if not, it's unjust.

294
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:28,000
So let's, in order to fix ideas

295
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
for this discussion,

296
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:38,000
take an actual example.

297
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Who's the wealthiest person

298
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:41,000
in the United States?

299
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:45,000
Wealthiest person in the world?

300
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Bill Gates.

301
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:47,000
It is.

302
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
That's right.

303
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Here he is.

304
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
You'd be happy too.

305
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:58,000
Now, what's his net worth?

306
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Anybody have any idea?

307
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:04,000
That's a big number.

308
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:05,000
During the Clinton years,

309
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,000
remember there was a controversy,

310
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:09,000
donors, big campaign contributors

311
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
were invited to stay overnight

312
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,000
in the Lincoln bedroom

313
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
at the White House.

314
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
I think if you contributed $25,000 or above,

315
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,000
someone figured out

316
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
that the median contribution

317
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
that got you invited to stay

318
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,000
a night in the Lincoln bedroom,

319
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Bill Gates could afford

320
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:29,000
to stay in the Lincoln bedroom

321
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
every night for the next 66,000 years.

322
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
Somebody else figured out

323
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
how much does he get paid

324
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
on an hourly basis?

325
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
And so they figured out

326
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:49,000
since he began Microsoft,

327
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
suppose he worked, what,

328
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
14 hours per day.

329
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
Reasonable guess.

330
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
And you calculate this net wealth.

331
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:03,000
It turns out that his rate of pay

332
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:08,000
is over $150,

333
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
not per hour,

334
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
not per minute.

335
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
$150, more than $150 per second.

336
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
Which means,

337
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
which means that if on a daily basis,

338
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:25,000
that if on his way to the office,

339
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Gates noticed a $100 bill on the street,

340
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:31,000
it wouldn't be worth his time

341
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
to stop and pick it up.

342
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:40,000
Now, most of you will say,

343
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
someone that wealthy,

344
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
surely we can tax them

345
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:49,000
to meet the pressing needs

346
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
of people who lack an education

347
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
or lack enough to eat

348
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
or lack decent housing.

349
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
They need it more than he does.

350
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
And if you were a utilitarian,

351
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:04,000
what would you do?

352
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
What tax policy would you have?

353
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
You'd redistribute in a flash,

354
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
wouldn't you?

355
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Because you would know,

356
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
being a good utilitarian,

357
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
that taking some,

358
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
a small amount,

359
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
he's scarcely going to notice it.

360
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
But it will make a huge improvement

361
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:25,000
in the lives and in the welfare

362
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
of those at the bottom.

363
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
But remember,

364
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:35,000
the libertarian theory says,

365
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
we can't just add up

366
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
and aggregate preferences

367
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
and satisfactions that way.

368
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:44,000
We have to respect persons,

369
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
and if he earned that money fairly,

370
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:49,000
without violating anybody else's rights,

371
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
in accordance with the two principles

372
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:52,000
of justice in acquisition

373
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
and justice in transfer,

374
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
then it would be wrong,

375
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
it would be a form of coercion

376
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
to take it away.

377
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
Michael Jordan is not as wealthy

378
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
as Bill Gates,

379
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:08,000
but he did pretty well for himself.

380
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
You want to see Michael Jordan?

381
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:13,000
There he is.

382
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:16,000
His income alone in one year

383
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
was 31 million dollars,

384
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,000
and then he made another 47 million dollars

385
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
in endorsements for Nike

386
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and other companies.

387
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
So his income was,

388
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:30,000
in one year, 78 million.

389
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
To require him to pay,

390
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
let's say, a third of his earnings

391
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
to the government

392
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:39,000
to support good causes,

393
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
like food and healthcare

394
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
and housing and education for the poor,

395
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
that's coercion.

396
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
That's unjust.

397
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:50,000
That violates his rights.

398
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:56,000
And that's why redistribution is wrong.

399
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:59,000
Now, how many agree with that argument?

400
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
Agree with the libertarian argument

401
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:04,000
that redistribution for the sake of

402
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:08,000
trying to help the poor is wrong?

403
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
And how many disagree with that argument?

404
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:16,000
All right, let's begin with those who disagree.

405
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:20,000
What's wrong with the libertarian case

406
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:26,000
against redistribution?

407
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:29,000
Yes.

408
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:31,000
I think these people like Michael Jordan

409
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:32,000
have received,

410
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
we're talking about working within a society,

411
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
and they received a larger gift from the society,

412
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:39,000
and they have a larger obligation in return

413
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,000
to give that through redistribution.

414
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
You know, you can say that Michael Jordan

415
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
may work just as hard as someone who works

416
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,000
doing laundry 12 hours, 14 hours a day,

417
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
but he's receiving more.

418
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,000
I don't think it's fair to say that, you know,

419
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:59,000
it's all on his, you know, inherent hard work.

420
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:00,000
All right.

421
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:04,000
Let's hear from defenders of libertarianism.

422
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
Why would it be wrong in principle

423
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,000
to tax the rich to help the poor?

424
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Go ahead.

425
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
My name's Joe, and I collect skateboards.

426
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
I've since bought 100 skateboards.

427
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
I live in a society of 100 people.

428
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
I'm the only one with skateboards.

429
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
Suddenly everyone decides they want a skateboard.

430
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
They come into my house, they take my,

431
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
they take 99 of my skateboards.

432
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
I think that is unjust.

433
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
Now, I think in certain circumstances,

434
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,000
it becomes necessary to overlook that injustice,

435
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
perhaps condone that injustice,

436
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:35,500
as in the case of the

437
00:19:35,500 --> 00:19:37,000
cabin boy being killed for food.

438
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
If people are on the verge of dying,

439
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
perhaps it is necessary to overlook that injustice.

440
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
But I think it's important to keep in mind

441
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
that we're still committing injustice

442
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
by taking people's belongings or assets.

443
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
Are you saying that taxing Michael Jordan,

444
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:54,000
say, at a 33% tax rate,

445
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
for good causes,

446
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
to feed the hungry,

447
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
is theft?

448
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:02,000
I think it's unjust.

449
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,000
Yes, I do believe it's theft,

450
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:07,000
but perhaps it is necessary to condone that theft.

451
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:08,000
But it's theft.

452
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
Yes.

453
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:19,000
Why is it theft, Joe?

454
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:20,000
Because...

455
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:23,000
Why is it like your collection of skateboards?

456
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:27,000
It's theft because, at least in my opinion,

457
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
and by the libertarian opinion,

458
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
he earned that money fairly,

459
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
and it belongs to him.

460
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:36,000
So to take it from him is, by definition, theft.

461
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:42,000
All right, let's see if there's...

462
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
Who wants to reply to Joe?

463
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Yes, go ahead.

464
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
I don't think this is necessarily a case

465
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
in which you have 99 skateboards

466
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,000
and the government...

467
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:52,000
Or you have 100 skateboards

468
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
and the government's taking 99 of them.

469
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
It's like you have more skateboards

470
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
than there are days in a year.

471
00:20:58,000 --> 00:20:59,000
You have more skateboards

472
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:00,000
than you're going to be able to use

473
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
in your entire lifetime,

474
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
and the government is taking part of those.

475
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,000
And I think that if you're operating in a society

476
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
in which the government's...

477
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
In which the government does not have the money

478
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:12,000
to pay for the skateboards,

479
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
then you're not taking part of those.

480
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
And if the government doesn't redistribute wealth,

481
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
then that allows for people to amass so much wealth

482
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
that people who haven't started

483
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
from this very...

484
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:26,000
The equal footing in our hypothetical situation

485
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
that doesn't exist in our real society

486
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,000
get undercut for the rest of their lives.

487
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
So you're worried that if there isn't

488
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
some degree of redistribution,

489
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
if some are left at the bottom,

490
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:41,000
there will be no genuine equality of opportunity.

491
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
All right.

492
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:47,000
The idea that taxation is theft.

493
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:50,000
Nozick takes that point one step further.

494
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:53,000
He agrees that it's theft.

495
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
He's more demanding than Joe.

496
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
Joe says it is theft.

497
00:21:57,000 --> 00:22:01,000
Maybe in an extreme case it's justified.

498
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:04,000
Maybe a parent is justified

499
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
in stealing a loaf of bread

500
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:09,000
to feed his or her hungry family.

501
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
So Joe is a...

502
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,000
What would you call yourself?

503
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
A compassionate quasi-libertarian?

504
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:20,000
Nozick says, if you think about it,

505
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:26,000
taxation amounts to the taking of earnings.

506
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
In other words, it means

507
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:38,000
taking the fruits of my labor.

508
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:41,000
But if the state has the right

509
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:45,000
to take my earnings or the fruits of my labor,

510
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,000
isn't that morally the same

511
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:52,000
as according to the state

512
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:57,000
the right to claim a portion of my labor?

513
00:22:57,000 --> 00:23:04,000
So taxation actually

514
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:08,000
is morally equivalent

515
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
to forced labor.

516
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
Because forced labor

517
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:16,000
involves the taking of my leisure,

518
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,000
my time, my efforts,

519
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,000
just as taxation takes the earnings

520
00:23:23,000 --> 00:23:27,000
that I make with my labor.

521
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
And so for Nozick

522
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:35,000
and for the libertarians,

523
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
taxation for redistribution is theft,

524
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:39,000
as Joe says.

525
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:43,000
But not only theft,

526
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:45,000
it's morally equivalent

527
00:23:45,000 --> 00:23:49,000
to laying claim to certain hours

528
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:53,000
of a person's life and labor.

529
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,000
So it's morally equivalent

530
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
to forced labor.

531
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:57,000
If the state has a right

532
00:23:57,000 --> 00:23:59,000
to claim the fruits of my labor,

533
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
that implies that it really

534
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
has an entitlement

535
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
to my labor itself.

536
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
And what is forced labor?

537
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:11,000
Forced labor,

538
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:13,000
Nozick points out,

539
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:14,000
is what?

540
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
Is slavery.

541
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:21,000
Because if I don't have the right,

542
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:22,000
the sole right,

543
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
to my own labor,

544
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:28,000
then that's really to say

545
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
that the government

546
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
or the political community

547
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
is a part owner in me.

548
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
And what does it mean

549
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:39,000
for the state to be a part owner in me?

550
00:24:39,000 --> 00:24:41,000
If you think about it,

551
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:44,000
it means that I'm a slave,

552
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
that I don't own myself.

553
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:51,000
So what this line of reasoning brings us to

554
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:55,000
is the fundamental principle

555
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:59,000
that underlies the libertarian case for rights.

556
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,000
What is that principle?

557
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:05,000
It's the idea that I own myself.

558
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:08,000
It's the idea of self-possession.

559
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:11,000
If you want to take rights seriously,

560
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,000
if you don't want to just regard people

561
00:25:13,000 --> 00:25:16,000
as collections of preferences,

562
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:19,000
the fundamental moral idea

563
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:22,000
to which you will be led

564
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:25,000
is the idea that we are the owners

565
00:25:25,000 --> 00:25:29,000
or the proprietors of our own person.

566
00:25:29,000 --> 00:25:31,000
And that's why

567
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:33,000
utilitarianism is so important.

568
00:25:33,000 --> 00:25:34,000
And that's why

569
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:38,000
utilitarianism goes wrong.

570
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,000
And that's why it's wrong

571
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:42,000
to yank the organs from that healthy patient.

572
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
You're acting as if

573
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:45,000
that patient belongs to you

574
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:47,000
or to the community.

575
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:50,000
But we belong to ourselves.

576
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:53,000
And that's the same reason

577
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,000
that it's wrong to make laws

578
00:25:55,000 --> 00:25:57,000
to protect us from ourselves

579
00:25:57,000 --> 00:25:59,000
or to tell us how to live,

580
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:01,000
to tell us what virtues

581
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:03,000
we should be governed by,

582
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:05,000
and that's also why it's wrong

583
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:07,000
to tax the rich,

584
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:08,000
to help the poor,

585
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
even for good causes,

586
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
even to help those who are displaced

587
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
by the Hurricane Katrina.

588
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:17,000
Ask them to give charity.

589
00:26:17,000 --> 00:26:19,000
But if you tax them,

590
00:26:19,000 --> 00:26:21,000
it's like forcing them to labor.

591
00:26:21,000 --> 00:26:23,000
Could you tell Michael Jordan

592
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:25,000
he has to skip the next week's games

593
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:28,000
and go down to help the people

594
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
displaced by Hurricane Katrina?

595
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
Morally, it's the same.

596
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:37,000
So the stakes are very high.

597
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:42,000
So far we've heard some objections

598
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
to the libertarian argument,

599
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
but if you want to reject it,

600
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:49,000
you have to break into this chain of reasoning,

601
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:51,000
which goes,

602
00:26:51,000 --> 00:26:55,000
taking my earnings is like taking my labor,

603
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:59,000
but taking my labor is making me a slave.

604
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:02,000
And if you disagree with that,

605
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:06,000
you must believe in the principle of self-possession.

606
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:09,000
Those who disagree,

607
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:11,000
gather your objections,

608
00:27:11,000 --> 00:27:13,000
and we'll begin with them next time.

609
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
Anyone like to take up that point?

610
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:25,000
Yes.

611
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:28,000
I feel like when you live in a society,

612
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:29,000
you give up that right.

613
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:30,000
I mean, technically,

614
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
if I want to personally give up that right,

615
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:33,000
I can't go out and kill someone

616
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:34,000
because they offend me.

617
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:35,000
That is self-possession.

618
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:36,000
Because I live in a society,

619
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:37,000
I cannot do that.

620
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:40,000
Victoria, are you questioning

621
00:27:40,000 --> 00:27:43,000
the fundamental premise of self-possession?

622
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:44,000
Yes.

623
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
I think that you don't really have self-possession

624
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
if you choose to live in a society

625
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
because you cannot just discount

626
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
the people around you.

627
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:55,000
We were talking last time about libertarianism.

628
00:27:57,000 --> 00:27:59,000
I want to go back to the arguments

629
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:01,000
for and against the redistribution

630
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,000
of income.

631
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
But before we do that,

632
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:10,000
just one word about the minimal state.

633
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
Milton Friedman,

634
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
the libertarian economist,

635
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:18,000
he points out that many of the functions

636
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
that we take for granted

637
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,000
as properly belonging to government

638
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,000
don't.

639
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:26,000
They are paternalist.

640
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:29,000
One example he gives is social security.

641
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:30,000
He says it's a good thing

642
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
to have a social security system.

643
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
He says it's a good idea

644
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:37,000
for people to save for their retirement

645
00:28:37,000 --> 00:28:39,000
during their earning years.

646
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:41,000
But it's wrong.

647
00:28:41,000 --> 00:28:44,000
It's a violation of people's liberty

648
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:48,000
for the government to force everyone,

649
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:51,000
whether they want to or not,

650
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:55,000
to put aside some earnings today

651
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,000
for the sake of their retirement.

652
00:28:57,000 --> 00:28:59,000
If people want to take the chance,

653
00:28:59,000 --> 00:29:01,000
or if people want to live big today

654
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:05,000
and live a poor retirement,

655
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:07,000
that should be their choice.

656
00:29:07,000 --> 00:29:10,000
They should be free to make those judgments

657
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
and take those risks.

658
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
So even social security

659
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:17,000
would still be at odds with the minimal state

660
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:20,000
that Milton Friedman argued for.

661
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
It's sometimes thought that

662
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
collective goods like police protection

663
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:27,000
and fire protection

664
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:30,000
will inevitably create the problem

665
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:31,000
of free riders,

666
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:36,000
unless they're publicly provided.

667
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:39,000
But there are ways

668
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:42,000
to prevent free riders.

669
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
There are ways to restrict

670
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
even seemingly collective goods

671
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:49,000
like fire protection.

672
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:51,000
I read an article a while back

673
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:53,000
about a private fire company,

674
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:57,000
the Salem Fire Corporation in Arkansas.

675
00:29:57,000 --> 00:30:00,000
You can sign up with the Salem Fire Corporation,

676
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
pay a yearly subscription fee,

677
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,000
and if your house catches on fire,

678
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:07,000
they will come and put out the fire.

679
00:30:07,000 --> 00:30:11,000
But they won't put out everybody's fire.

680
00:30:11,000 --> 00:30:13,000
They will only put it out

681
00:30:13,000 --> 00:30:18,000
if it's a fire in the home of a subscriber,

682
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
or if it starts to spread

683
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:23,000
and to threaten the home of a subscriber.

684
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:26,000
The newspaper article told the story

685
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:29,000
of a homeowner who had subscribed

686
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:32,000
to this company in the past,

687
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
but failed to renew his subscription.

688
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,000
His house caught on fire.

689
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:39,000
The Salem Fire Corporation showed up with its trucks

690
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:41,000
and watched the house burn,

691
00:30:41,000 --> 00:30:45,000
just making sure that it didn't spread.

692
00:30:45,000 --> 00:30:47,000
The fire chief was asked,

693
00:30:47,000 --> 00:30:49,000
well, he wasn't exactly the fire chief,

694
00:30:49,000 --> 00:30:52,000
I guess he was the CEO.

695
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:53,000
He was asked,

696
00:30:53,000 --> 00:30:55,000
how can you stand by with fire equipment

697
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:58,000
and allow a person's home to burn?

698
00:30:58,000 --> 00:30:59,000
He replied,

699
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:01,000
once we verified there was no danger

700
00:31:01,000 --> 00:31:03,000
to a member's property,

701
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:05,000
we had no choice but to back off

702
00:31:05,000 --> 00:31:07,000
according to our rules.

703
00:31:07,000 --> 00:31:09,000
If we responded to all fires, he said,

704
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:12,000
there would be no incentive to subscribe.

705
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:14,000
The homeowner, in this case,

706
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,000
tried to renew his subscription

707
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:19,000
at the scene of the fire,

708
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
but the head of the company refused.

709
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:23,000
You can't wreck your car, he said,

710
00:31:23,000 --> 00:31:25,000
and then buy insurance for it.

711
00:31:25,000 --> 00:31:26,000
He said,

712
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
I'll buy it later.

713
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:31,000
So even public goods that we take for granted

714
00:31:31,000 --> 00:31:34,000
as being within the proper province of government

715
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,000
can, many of them, in principle,

716
00:31:36,000 --> 00:31:41,000
be isolated, made exclusive to those who pay.

717
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:45,000
That's all to do with the question of collective goods

718
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:50,000
and the libertarian's injunction against paternalism.

719
00:31:50,000 --> 00:31:52,000
But let's go back now

720
00:31:52,000 --> 00:31:55,000
to the arguments about redistribution.

721
00:31:55,000 --> 00:32:00,000
Now, underlying the libertarian's case

722
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:04,000
for the minimal state

723
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:06,000
is a worry about coercion.

724
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:09,000
But what's wrong with coercion?

725
00:32:09,000 --> 00:32:14,000
The libertarian offers this answer.

726
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:17,000
To coerce someone,

727
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:19,000
to use some person

728
00:32:19,000 --> 00:32:23,000
for the sake of the general welfare,

729
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:24,000
is wrong

730
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:27,000
because it calls into question

731
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:29,000
the fundamental fact

732
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:32,000
that we own ourselves,

733
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:34,000
the fundamental moral fact

734
00:32:34,000 --> 00:32:38,000
of self-possession or self-ownership.

735
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:44,000
The libertarian's argument against redistribution

736
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:46,000
begins with this fundamental idea

737
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:49,000
that we own ourselves.

738
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,000
Nozick says

739
00:32:51,000 --> 00:32:53,000
that if

740
00:32:53,000 --> 00:32:55,000
the society as a whole

741
00:32:55,000 --> 00:32:57,000
can go to Bill Gates

742
00:32:57,000 --> 00:32:59,000
or go to Michael Jordan

743
00:32:59,000 --> 00:33:03,000
and tax away a portion of their wealth,

744
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:07,000
what the society is really asserting

745
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:10,000
is a collective property right

746
00:33:10,000 --> 00:33:13,000
in Bill Gates

747
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:15,000
or in Michael Jordan.

748
00:33:15,000 --> 00:33:18,000
But that violates

749
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:20,000
the fundamental principle

750
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:22,000
that we belong to ourselves.

751
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
Now, we've already heard

752
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
a number of objections

753
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:30,000
to the libertarian argument.

754
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:32,000
What I would like to do today

755
00:33:32,000 --> 00:33:36,000
is to give

756
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:38,000
the libertarians among us

757
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:41,000
a chance to answer the objections

758
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
that have been raised.

759
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:44,000
And some have been,

760
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:47,000
some have already identified themselves

761
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:51,000
and have agreed to come and make the case

762
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:52,000
for libertarianism,

763
00:33:52,000 --> 00:33:54,000
to reply to the objections

764
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:56,000
that have been raised.

765
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:57,000
So, raise your hand

766
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:59,000
if you are among the libertarians

767
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:01,000
who's prepared to stand up

768
00:34:01,000 --> 00:34:03,000
for the theory

769
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:05,000
and respond to the objections.

770
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:07,000
You are?

771
00:34:07,000 --> 00:34:08,000
Alex Harris,

772
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:11,000
